chunk_findings.detail:
`_append_line` no longer performs the line as a single `write(2)` call — it now goes through `_write_all`, which loops `os.write(fd, view)` on an `O_APPEND` descriptor. The `O_APPEND` offset-update is atomic per *syscall*, not per *line*, so once the line is written in more than one call the "whole lines are appended" guarantee in the new README text (and the "the file's integrity does not depend on it [the lock]" claim) is false, and the old "concurrent appenders never interleave inside a line" comment was silently dropped because it no longer holds. Concrete interleaving: process A appends `{"at":...}
`; its first `os.write` returns a short count (the exact case this change was added to handle — e.g. ENOSPC / interrupted write), so `view` is re-sliced and a second `os.write` is issued. Between those two calls process B (which, per the README, need not be excluded by the lock) appends its complete `{"at":...}
`. The bytes on disk become `A_partialB_full
A_rest
`: the two lines are fused into one invalid-JSON line, so the "corrupt line is skipped" logic discards B's perfectly valid sample along with A's. The failure mode is a lost sample for a concurrent agent, and the documented lock-free integrity property is what makes it reachable.

